-operation. In the persistent remote privileged shell:
+Before taking the deployment lock, pause the installed backup timers and drain
+both backup jobs. In the same persistent privileged shell, remember only timers
+that were active so an intentionally disabled installation stays disabled:
+
+```bash
+PLIEGO_BACKUP_TIMERS_TO_RESUME=()
+for timer in pliego-backup.timer object-backup.timer pliego-backup-freshness.timer; do
+  if systemctl is-active --quiet "$timer"; then
+    PLIEGO_BACKUP_TIMERS_TO_RESUME+=("$timer")
+    systemctl stop "$timer"
+  fi
+done
+PLIEGO_BACKUP_DRAIN_DEADLINE=$((SECONDS + 3600))
+for unit in pliego-backup.service object-backup.service; do
+  while :; do
+    PLIEGO_BACKUP_UNIT_STATE=$(systemctl show "$unit" --property=ActiveState --value) || exit 1
+    case "$PLIEGO_BACKUP_UNIT_STATE" in
+      inactive|failed) break ;;
+      active|activating|deactivating|reloading) ;;
+      *) printf '%s\n' 'Unexpected backup state; leave timers paused and investigate.' >&2; exit 1 ;;
+    esac
+    if (( SECONDS >= PLIEGO_BACKUP_DRAIN_DEADLINE )); then
+      printf '%s\n' 'Backup drain timed out; do not kill the job or bypass its lock.' >&2
+      exit 1
+    fi
+    sleep 5
+  done
+done
+```
+
+A missing/uninstalled unit may report inactive. Stop on other lookup errors.
+Do not cancel a working dump. Both jobs retain the existing deployment-lock inode
+through systemd OpenFile; contention can mean either backup or another operator.
+Never remove, truncate outside the existing runbook open, replace, remode or bypass
